# The discover safety valve (docs/03, machine.DISCOVER_MAX_TURNS). An evasive caller who answers nothing
# must NOT trap the call in discovery forever: the bail is `stage_turn_count > DISCOVER_MAX_TURNS`,
# and stage_turn_count resets to 0 on entering discover — so it takes one turn MORE than the
# budget to escape. The off-by-one is the whole reason this script is worth pinning.
# The budget is `len(DISCOVERY_ORDER)` — one turn per slot — so it grew from four to five when
# the name became the first slot (Roma is inbound; nobody tells us who rang), and the escape
# moved one turn later with it. If DISCOVERY_ORDER changes again, this fixture is what notices.
# lead_name is null here on purpose: this caller dodges the name too, which is the realistic
# inbound version of "answers nothing" and exercises the attempt cap on the very first slot.
# No lock at the end, and that is correct rather than a failure: the valve exists to stop
# the call being stuck, not to rescue it.
# The "dekhta hoon" turn lands in objection, not pivot. That is the soft-no docs/02 names as the thing
# a win is NOT, and it is now classified from structure — objections used to be detected only in
# pivot/objection/close, so a stall during the structure pitch went unanswered and the call carried on as
# if the lead had agreed. It routes back to pivot on the next turn, which the last turn pins.
